<h2><strong>FAQs</strong></h2>
<h3><strong>Are patio pavers strong enough for cars?</strong></h3>
<p>Some patio pavers may support occasional vehicle use, but standard options are not always designed for repeated traffic. Choosing the correct material and installing a strong foundation is essential to prevent cracking and shifting.</p>
<h3><strong>What is the difference between patio pavers and driveway pavers?</strong></h3>
<p>Patio pavers are mainly designed for foot traffic, while driveway pavers are built to withstand heavier vehicle loads. Driveway pavers are typically thicker, stronger, and installed with deeper base preparation.</p>
<h3><strong>How thick should driveway pavers be?</strong></h3>
<p>Most driveway pavers are generally thicker than patio pavers because they need to support vehicle weight. The exact thickness depends on the material, traffic level, and manufacturer recommendations.</p>
<h3><strong>Do paver driveways last longer than concrete driveways?</strong></h3>
<p>A properly installed paver driveway can provide excellent durability and easier repairs compared to concrete. Individual pavers can be replaced if damaged, making maintenance simpler over the long term.</p>

<h2><strong>Final Thoughts</strong></h2>
<p>Patio pavers can sometimes be used for driveway applications, but choosing the right type of paver is essential for achieving long-term performance. Driveway-rated materials, proper foundation preparation, and effective drainage planning all play an important role in preventing issues such as cracking, shifting, and surface damage.</p>
<p>Using unsuitable pavers or skipping important installation steps can lead to costly repairs and reduced driveway lifespan. Professional planning and installation help ensure the surface remains durable, attractive, and capable of handling Colorado weather conditions.</p>
